    "https://hr.uconn.edu/adjunct-faculty-opportunities/": {
      "title": "UConn Cookie Information",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty Opportunities | Human Resources | University of Connecticut Skip to Navigation Skip to UConn Search Skip to Content Our websites may use cookies to personalize and enhance your experience. By continuing without changing your cookie settings, you agree to this collection. For more information, please see our University Websites Privacy Notice . UConn Cookie Information What are Cookies? Authentication Cookies Analytics Cookies Opt Out Web cookies (also called HTTP cookies , browser cookies , or simply cookies ) are small pieces of data that websites store on your device (computer, phone, etc.) through your web browser. They are used to remember information about you and your interactions with the site. Purpose of Cookies: Session Management : Keeping you logged in Remembering items in a shopping cart Saving language or theme preferences Personalization : Tailoring content or ads based on your previous activity Tracking & Analytics : Monitoring browsing behavior for analytics or marketing purposes Types of Cookies: Session Cookies : Temporary; deleted when you close your browser Used for things like keeping you logged in during a single session Persistent Cookies : Stored on your device until they expire or are manually deleted Used for remembering login credentials, settings, etc. First-Party Cookies : Set by the website you're visiting directly Third-Party Cookies : Set by other domains (usually advertisers) embedded in the website Commonly used for tracking across multiple sites Authentication cookies are a special type of web cookie used to identify and verify a user after they log in to a website or web application. What They Do: Once you log in to a site, the server creates an authentication cookie and sends it to your browser. This cookie: Proves to the website that you're logged in Prevents you from having to log in again on every page you visit Can persist across sessions if you select \"Remember me\" What's Inside an Authentication Cookie? Typically, it contains: A unique session ID (not your actual password) Optional metadata (e.g., expiration time, security flags) Analytics cookies are cookies used to collect data about how visitors interact with a website . Their primary purpose is to help website owners understand and improve user experience by analyzing things like: How users navigate the site Which pages are most/least visited How long users stay on each page What device, browser, or location the user is from What They Track: Some examples of data analytics cookies may collect: Page views and time spent on pages Click paths (how users move from page to page) Bounce rate (users who leave without interacting) User demographics (location, language, device) Referring websites (how users arrived at the site) Here’s how you can disable cookies in common browsers: 1. Google Chrome Open Chrome and click the three vertical dots in the top-right corner. Go to Settings > Privacy and security > Cookies and other site data . Choose your preferred option: Block all cookies (not recommended, can break most websites). Block third-party cookies (can block ads and tracking cookies). 2. Mozilla Firefox Open Firefox and click the three horizontal lines in the top-right corner. Go to Settings > Privacy & Security . Under the Enhanced Tracking Protection section, choose Strict to block most cookies or Custom to manually choose which cookies to block. 3. Safari Open Safari and click Safari in the top-left corner of the screen. Go to Preferences > Privacy . Check Block all cookies to stop all cookies, or select options to block third-party cookies. 4. Microsoft Edge Open Edge and click the three horizontal dots in the top-right corner. Go to Settings > Privacy, search, and services > Cookies and site permissions . Select your cookie settings from there, including blocking all cookies or blocking third-party cookies. 5. On Mobile (iOS/Android) For Safari on iOS: Go to Settings > Safari > Privacy & Security > Block All Cookies . For Chrome on Android: Open the app, tap the three dots, go to Settings > Privacy and security > Cookies . Be Aware: Disabling cookies can make your online experience more difficult. Some websites may not load properly, or you may be logged out frequently. Also, certain features may not work as expected. UConn University of Connecticut Search University of Connecticut Search UConn A to Z Index UConn A to Z Index Site A-Z UConn A-Z Human Resources Search this site search Search Human Resources Skip to content Home UConn Jobs HR Services Employee Accommodations Employee Assistance Program Employment Verifications Faculty Travel Funding Guidelines HR Workforce Data Leaving UConn & Exit Interviews Personnel Files Update Personal Information Benefits & Leaves Benefits Employee Benefits Overview Health Benefits Retirement Benefits Paid Time Off Benefits Supplemental Benefits Tuition Benefits Life Events Impacting Benefits & Beneficiaries Planning for Retirement Absence Management Family & Medical Leaves of Absence Other Leaves of Absence Workers’ Compensation Telecommuting and Flexible Schedules Engagement & Learning Employee Experience Survey Employee Recognition UConn Spirit Awards Years of Service Employee Wellness Learning@Work Onboarding & Orientations Organizational and Staff Development UConn’s Workforce Recruitment Advertising a Position PageUp Offer Letters Faculty Hiring Special Payroll Information Background Checks Classification & Compensation Classification and Compensation Career Paths (UCPEA) Career Progression (UCPEA) Faculty Titles Dictionary Fair Labor Standards Act (FLSA) UCPEA Compensatory Time Performance UCPEA Performance Evaluations Classified Performance Appraisals Management/Confidential Employee Performance Management Resolving Employee Performance Problems Updating the University Organizational Structure Labor Relations Labor Contracts & Employment Law Postings International Employees Employees of Concern Team Applying UConn’s Emergency Closing Policy Contact Us Search this site search Search Adjunct Faculty Opportunities Please Note: All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. 2026 Adjunct Faculty – Department of Educational Psychology, Research Methods, Measurement, and Evaluation (RMME) Program – Posted August 27, 2026 Adjunct Faculty – Department of Educational Psychology, Research Methods, Measurement, and Evaluation (RMME) Program The UConn Department of Educational Psychology within the Neag School of Education seeks academically and professionally qualified instructors to serve as adjunct faculty for a variety of courses offered by our Research Methods, Measurement, and Evaluation (RMME) program each semester. We are seeking adjunct faculty who have experience teaching asynchronous online for a variety of Core courses and EPSY Elective courses offered by the RMME program. Adjunct faculty need is based on scheduling and enrollment each semester and actual course offerings may vary each semester. Course descriptions for both Core courses and EPSY Elective courses offered by the RMME program can be found here . More information on the RMME program can be found here. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Earned Ph.D. in research methods, measurement, evaluation, statistics, or a related field. Research interests or applied experience in educational domains (i.e., PK-12 and/or Higher Education). At least two years post-Ph.D. research or applied experience in research methods, measurement, evaluation, statistics, or a related field. Demonstrated potential to teach graduate level classes in research methods, measurement, evaluation, or statistics. Ability to fulfill administrative requirements in a reliable and timely manner. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Prior teaching experience, especially in online asynchronous formats. Prior experience with Blackboard Ultra or another learning management system. A record of or demonstrated potential for quality teaching. Experience and real-world use of concepts covered in course content. Experience and expertise in statistical programming (e.g., R, Python, SPSS). APPOINTMENT TERMS This is a temporary position for a period of one semester, with the possibility of extension depending upon teaching quality, program needs, and available funding. Applicants will be contacted by the program as needed. Please note that preference will be given to qualified CT candidates. Minimum compensation for this position is established via collective bargaining unions. Current rates can be reviewed here . TO APPLY Please submit your application here via the RMME Program’s Adjunct Instructors page. The form will ask you to submit a cover letter and CV/resume . Your cover letter should indicate which online RMME classes you would like to teach for our program and briefly describe your qualifications. Your CV/resume should include information about your work and educational background, and other experiences that represent the breadth of your professional accomplishments. If you have questions, please contact our team at Methods@uconn.edu . This post serves as an “open pool” where applicants will be contacted based on the availability and scheduling needs of courses. Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression. The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. (HR26-32) Adjunct Faculty – Department of Agricultural and Resource Economics (ARE), UConn Storrs – Posted July 29, 2026 Adjunct Faculty – Department of Agricultural and Resource Economics (ARE) The Department of Agricultural and Resource Economics (ARE) at the University of Connecticut invites applications for an adjunct faculty position to teach undergraduate or graduate-level courses beginning January 2027. We are currently seeking an instructor (s) for courses related to: Cost-Benefit Analysis and Applications, Essentials of Accounting and Business, A foundational AI intro course, and Data Ethics in the Era of Misinformation Adjunct faculty typically teach one or two courses per semester and are compensated on a per-credit basis. These courses are taught in person on the Storrs campus. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Master’s degree or equivalent professional experience in applied economics, economics, business, finance, data science, or a related field. Experience working effectively with diverse populations. Demonstrated verbal and written communication skills. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Ph.D. in a relevant field. Previous teaching experience in related courses. Experience teaching in academic or professional settings. APPOINTMENT TERMS These are temporary positions (teaching one or more courses) for a period of one semester, with the possibility of re-hire depending upon department/program needs and available funding. Successful applicants will be contacted to schedule an interview. Compensation follows UConn’s Adjunct Per Credit Rates . TO APPLY Please submit a curriculum vitae , the names and contact information of three professional references, and a cover letter indicating the following: which course(s) you seek to teach; what general subject areas you feel qualified to teach; your general availability (days, time of day) for teaching. These items and any other relevant materials (e.g., sample syllabi, teaching evaluations) should be submitted via this link . This is a pool posting: Please reference Search #494096 in your application materials. Screening of applications will begin immediately and will continue until positions are filled. Qualified applicants may be offered positions in future semesters. Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime