l employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same.  All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, national origin, genetics, disability, age, or veteran status. The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO Employer. (HR25-48) Adjunct Faculty, Anthropology – UConn Stamford – Posted October 28, 2025 Adjunct Faculty Position Anthropology Faculty, UConn Stamford The UConn Stamford campus has two adjunct openings for Spring 2026: ANTH 1000 Peoples and Cultures of the World – Tu/Th 12:30 pm-1:45 pm ANTH 1500 Great discoveries Archeology – Tu/Th 11:00 am-12:15 pm There is a possibility that the courses could be taught hybrid with one day a week in-person at the Stamford Campus and one day a week remote synchronous. Detailed Descriptions . MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS MA degree in Anthropology or related field. Experience teaching Anthropology. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Ph.D in Anthropology or related field. At least six semesters of teaching experience at the college level. APPOINTMENT TERMS These adjunct positions are temporary for a period of one semester, with the possibility of extension dependent upon program needs and available funding. Compensation for these positions will be $2,062 per credit, $6,186 per 3-credit course.  Adjuncts are permitted to teach up to eight credits per semester. TO APPLY Interested applicants should submit a letter of interest & c.v. indicating professional and related teaching experience to StamfordAcademicAffairs@Uconn.edu . Successful applicants will be contacted to schedule an interview. This post serves as an “open pool” where applicants will be contacted based on the availability and scheduling needs of courses. Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics, which may be found at https://portal.ct.gov/Ethics/Statutes-and-Regulations . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce.  The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner.  This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all.  We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same.  All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, national origin, genetics, disability, age, or veteran status. The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO Employer. [HR25-45] Adjunct Faculty (Special Payroll Lecturer) – UConn School of Law – Posted October 27, 2025 Adjunct Professor (Special Payroll Lecturer) UConn School of Law Position Overview The University of Connecticut School of Law invites applications for the position of Adjunct Professor to teach Liability Insurance. This upper-level elective provides a detailed examination of the legal principles governing liability insurance, with major emphasis on commercial general liability (CGL), homeowners, commercial and private auto, and umbrella/excess coverage. The course explores subtopics such as environmental impairment liability, coverage for intellectual property and employment claims, insurer litigation management guidelines, and common exclusions (including intentional acts). Students also examine the varying perspectives of the parties in insurance disputes — policyholders, insurers, brokers, and reinsurers — and the strategic considerations that shape litigation and settlement tactics in coverage disputes. The successful candidate will bring deep, practice-based expertise in insurance law, an ability to integrate doctrinal analysis with the realities of complex litigation and claims management, and demonstrate a commitment to academic rigor, professional ethics, and inclusive legal education . Duties and Responsibilities Teach Liability Insurance in accordance with the UConn Law curriculum, incorporating real-world developments in coverage litigation and regulatory trends. Develop a syllabus that emphasizes the principles of coverage interpretation, claims handling, and insurer/insured dynamics, while engaging students in problem-based learning. Guide discussion on complex topics including bad faith, allocation, exclusions, and emerging risks (e.g., cyber, environmental, and AI-related liabilities). Mentor and advise students pursuing careers in insurance, litigation, or regulatory practice. Hold regular office hours and evaluate student performance through written assignments, class participation, and examinations. Minimum Qualifications J.D. degree from an accredited law school. Admission to practice and  substantial experience in liability insurance coverage litigation, counseling, or arbitration. Demonstrated national recognition in the field of liability insurance law through litigation, scholarship, professional leadership, and/or public service. Deep familiarity with CGL and other liability policies, exclusions, and specialized coverage forms. Proven ability to teach and communicate complex legal and strategic issues clearly and effectively. Appellate experience and authorship of briefs on key liability insurance coverage issues. Preferred Qualifications Authorship of leading treatises, chapters, or amicus briefs in liability or coverage law. Involvement in national professional organizations such as the American College of Coverage Counsel or ABA Tort Trial & Insurance Practice Section. Recognition by national ranking entities (e.g., Chambers USA, Best Lawyers in America). Contributor to the ALI Restatement of the Law, Liability Insurance. Appointment Terms This is a temporary position for a period of one semester, with the possibility of extension depending upon performance, program needs, and available funding. Applicants will be contacted as needed. Please note, preference will be given to qualified local CT candidates.  This post serves as an “open pool” where applicants will be contacted based on the availability and scheduling needs of courses. The stipend for a 3-credit course is $4,500, or $2,250 if the course is co-taught. To Apply Please submit a resume, cover letter and contact information for three professional references to Travis Pantin ( travis.pantin@uconn.edu ) and Nicole Giaccio ( nicole.giaccio@uconn.edu ). Review of applications will begin immediately. If you have questions, please contact Travis Pantin ( travis.pantin@uconn.edu ) and Nicole Giaccio ( nicole.giaccio@uconn.edu ). Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics, which may be found at https://portal.ct.gov/Ethics/Statutes-and-Regulations . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce.  The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and b