      "title": "UConn Cookie Information",
      "description": "Temporary Job Openings | Human Resources | University of Connecticut Skip to Navigation Skip to UConn Search Skip to Content Our websites may use cookies to personalize and enhance your experience. By continuing without changing your cookie settings, you agree to this collection. For more information, please see our University Websites Privacy Notice . UConn Cookie Information What are Cookies? Authentication Cookies Analytics Cookies Opt Out Web cookies (also called HTTP cookies , browser cookies , or simply cookies ) are small pieces of data that websites store on your device (computer, phone, etc.) through your web browser. They are used to remember information about you and your interactions with the site. Purpose of Cookies: Session Management : Keeping you logged in Remembering items in a shopping cart Saving language or theme preferences Personalization : Tailoring content or ads based on your previous activity Tracking & Analytics : Monitoring browsing behavior for analytics or marketing purposes Types of Cookies: Session Cookies : Temporary; deleted when you close your browser Used for things like keeping you logged in during a single session Persistent Cookies : Stored on your device until they expire or are manually deleted Used for remembering login credentials, settings, etc. First-Party Cookies : Set by the website you're visiting directly Third-Party Cookies : Set by other domains (usually advertisers) embedded in the website Commonly used for tracking across multiple sites Authentication cookies are a special type of web cookie used to identify and verify a user after they log in to a website or web application. What They Do: Once you log in to a site, the server creates an authentication cookie and sends it to your browser. This cookie: Proves to the website that you're logged in Prevents you from having to log in again on every page you visit Can persist across sessions if you select \"Remember me\" What's Inside an Authentication Cookie? Typically, it contains: A unique session ID (not your actual password) Optional metadata (e.g., expiration time, security flags) Analytics cookies are cookies used to collect data about how visitors interact with a website . Their primary purpose is to help website owners understand and improve user experience by analyzing things like: How users navigate the site Which pages are most/least visited How long users stay on each page What device, browser, or location the user is from What They Track: Some examples of data analytics cookies may collect: Page views and time spent on pages Click paths (how users move from page to page) Bounce rate (users who leave without interacting) User demographics (location, language, device) Referring websites (how users arrived at the site) Here’s how you can disable cookies in common browsers: 1. Google Chrome Open Chrome and click the three vertical dots in the top-right corner. Go to Settings > Privacy and security > Cookies and other site data . Choose your preferred option: Block all cookies (not recommended, can break most websites). Block third-party cookies (can block ads and tracking cookies). 2. Mozilla Firefox Open Firefox and click the three horizontal lines in the top-right corner. Go to Settings > Privacy & Security . Under the Enhanced Tracking Protection section, choose Strict to block most cookies or Custom to manually choose which cookies to block. 3. Safari Open Safari and click Safari in the top-left corner of the screen. Go to Preferences > Privacy . Check Block all cookies to stop all cookies, or select options to block third-party cookies. 4. Microsoft Edge Open Edge and click the three horizontal dots in the top-right corner. Go to Settings > Privacy, search, and services > Cookies and site permissions . Select your cookie settings from there, including blocking all cookies or blocking third-party cookies. 5. On Mobile (iOS/Android) For Safari on iOS: Go to Settings > Safari > Privacy & Security > Block All Cookies . For Chrome on Android: Open the app, tap the three dots, go to Settings > Privacy and security > Cookies . Be Aware: Disabling cookies can make your online experience more difficult. Some websites may not load properly, or you may be logged out frequently. Also, certain features may not work as expected. UConn University of Connecticut Search University of Connecticut Search UConn A to Z Index UConn A to Z Index Site A-Z UConn A-Z Human Resources Search this site search Search Human Resources Skip to content Home UConn Jobs HR Services Employee Accommodations Employee Assistance Program Employment Verifications Faculty Travel Funding Guidelines HR Workforce Data Leaving UConn & Exit Interviews Personnel Files Update Personal Information Benefits & Leaves Benefits Employee Benefits Overview Health Benefits Retirement Benefits Paid Time Off Benefits Supplemental Benefits Tuition Benefits Life Events Impacting Benefits & Beneficiaries Planning for Retirement Absence Management Family & Medical Leaves of Absence Other Leaves of Absence Workers’ Compensation Telecommuting and Flexible Schedules Engagement & Learning Employee Experience Survey Employee Recognition UConn Spirit Awards Years of Service Employee Wellness Learning@Work Onboarding & Orientations Organizational and Staff Development UConn’s Workforce Recruitment Advertising a Position PageUp Offer Letters Faculty Hiring Special Payroll Information Background Checks Classification & Compensation Classification and Compensation Career Paths (UCPEA) Career Progression (UCPEA) Faculty Titles Dictionary Fair Labor Standards Act (FLSA) UCPEA Compensatory Time Performance UCPEA Performance Evaluations Classified Performance Appraisals Management/Confidential Employee Performance Management Resolving Employee Performance Problems Updating the University Organizational Structure Labor Relations Labor Contracts & Employment Law Postings International Employees Employees of Concern Team Applying UConn’s Emergency Closing Policy Contact Us Search this site search Search Temporary Job Openings PLEASE NOTE All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. 2026 Temporary Financial Assistant / Budget Analyst – CT Transportation Institute, Storrs Campus – Posted August 25, 2026 POSITION SUMMARY We are looking for a temporary Financial Assistant/Budget Analyst to provide financial, budgeting, administrative, and program support for the CT Training and Technical Assistance Program. This position is responsible for processing financial and payroll transactions, maintaining accurate financial records, monitoring budgets, preparing financial reports, and ensuring compliance with university and funding agency policies and procedures.  Experience with UCONN financial processes is a plus, but training will be provided. This position is expected to last approximately 6 -8 months. PRIMARY RESPONSIBILITIES Monitor and maintain center accounts including budgets, expenditures, and financial records. Process, review, and approve financial transactions. Manage invoices, verify purchase orders, and ensure availability of funds. Prepare financial reports and statements. Monitor financial activities for compliance, efficiency, and effectiveness. Respond to financial inquiries and resolve fiscal issues. Maintain confidentiality and ensure compliance with applicable policies and regulations. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Two to three years of related financial, budgeting, accounting, or administrative experience. Bachelor’s degree in accounting, finance, business administration, public administration, or a related field. Equivalent combinations of education and experience will be considered. Knowledge of accounting practices, budget preparation, fiscal controls, and financial record systems. Ability to interpret financial information and prepare accurate reports. Strong analytical, organizational, and problem-solving skills. Demonstrated proficiency with Microsoft Office (specifically including Excel) and related business software applications. Strong written and verbal communication skills. Ability to work independently and collaboratively with individuals from diverse backgrounds. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Familiarity with UCONN financial systems and processes Familiarity with financial regulations governing federal and university funds and sponsored programs. APPOINTMENT TERMS This is a temporary, part-time position working 20 to 35 hours per week, expected to last approximately 6-8 months. The hiring range for this position is $30 to $35 per hour, dependent on qualifications and experience. TO APPLY Please send a cover letter, resume and list of three professional references with contact information to mary.c.mccarthy@uconn.edu.  Review of applications will begin immediately. Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. HR26-31 Cheerleading Coach – Department of Student Activities University Spirit, Pride, Tradition Programs – Posted June 26, 2026 Cheerleading Coach Department of Student Activities University Spirit, Pride, Tradition Programs The Department of Student Activities (DSA) at the University of Connecticut announces the opening of a special payroll Cheerleading Coach. This position is responsible for assisting in the selection, training, performance, oversight, and evaluation of the University Cheer Team and assisting in the oversight and operation of the University Spirit, Pride, Tradition Program in a manner consistent with the University’s visions for a comprehensive program. About Student Activities We offer a diverse portfolio of educational, social, and recreational programs for the entire campus.  The related programs and services reach from the classroom and residence halls to the sidelines and beyond and engage students with unique programs and initiatives defined by their quality, creativity, and effectiveness. Student Activities staff are a progressive team of individuals, dedicated to the profession, prideful of our University, and committed to excellence. The Division Undergraduate Student Success and staff in the Department of Student Activities are committed to inclusion, equity and belonging; competence in working with culturally and ethnically diverse populations; and awareness of how issues of difference, power, and privilege manifest in higher education environments.  The ideal candidate promotes equity and inclusion by demonstrating and supporting an understanding, sensitivity, and appreciation for social justice, cultural humility, and inclusiveness. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Assist in the oversight of the University Cheer Team to ensure a safe and developmentally appropriate experience for students. Be familiar and compliant with all applicable policies and procedures related to the activities of the University Spirit, Pride Tradition Program, including but not limited to NCA, UCA, NCAA, conference, State, and University. Assist with the holding of try-outs annually and as needed to ensure high quality and diversity. Assist with the scheduling and execution of regular training/practice sessions; maintaining oversight of Cheer Team, and all SPT members as necessary at appearances and games. Work with University departments to ensure the health and safety of SPT members throughout the season; making referrals and following up as necessary. Assist in ensuring SPT member compliance with all program eligibility requirements. Assist with risk management strategies related to tryouts, practices, games, travel, etc. Assist in the oversight of the University SPT program to ensure performances/appearances are consistent with university image. Assist in coordinating efforts to create a program that actively engages fans. Assist in enforcing standards of conduct to be followed during all appearances. Accompany SPT members to university appearances as necessary. Assist in actively fostering a campus climate that is welcoming and supportive of University of Connecticut’s diverse student body and encourages communication with and among campus constituencies. Assist with the planning and implementation of related major program initiatives through weekly office hours. Assist with recruitment/outreach initiatives. Assist in the implementation of a comprehensive alumni relations program Assist in the coordination of programmatic public relations and promotion strategies Assist in daily program tasks as needed Meet with, and keep informed, Spirit Coordinator and other designated contact(s) in the Department of Student Activities as well as elsewhere on campus. Ability to actively foster a campus climate that is welcoming and supportive of University of Connecticut’s diverse student body and encourages communication with and among campus constituencies. Perform related duties as required. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree from an accredited college/university OR comparable experience. Demonstrated knowledge and/or experience as a member of a collegiate or professional cheer team. Demonstrated knowledge and/or experience selecting, training, teaching, and coaching collegiate cheer teams. Familiarity with relevant associations, USA Cheer, UCA/NCA, NCAA, and other relevant organization standards Excellent planning, organizational, oral, and written communication skills. Demonstrated people skills including the ability to work effectively with students, faculty, staff, and administrators in a diverse and multi-cultural higher education environment. Willingness to work irregular and flexible hours. APPOINTMENT TERMS This is a temporary, special payroll position with an hourly rate of $25.00 and a schedule of 25 hours per week. The start date for this position is anticipated to be July 24, 2026, and will continue until May 27, 2027, with the possibility of renewal. This position does not consist of a typical work-week schedule, and nights and weekends are to be expected. Practice, game times, and occasional office hours are subject to change. TO APPLY Please submit cover letter, resume and three professional references to Sheldron Fulcher, at Sheldron.fulcher@uconn.edu no later than July 15, 2026. Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. HR26-27 Temporary University Specialist – College of Engineering – Posted June 26, 2026 Temporary University Specialist College of Engineering The University of Connecticut, College of Engineering seeks academically and professionally qualified applicants for a part-time (temporary) position as a Temporary University Specialist in the Department of Biomedical Engineering (BME). The successful candidate will primarily contribute to the Department’s Clinical Engineering Internship Program. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Provide program support for the Clinical Engineering (CE) Internship Program. Respond to inquiries from students, staff, and others on matters relating to Clinical Engineering Internship Program procedures and policies, making referrals as appropriate. Serve as a liaison between CE students and the host hospitals in the program. Regularly communicates with the CE students and host hospitals and provides feedback to each group and the BME Department. Plan and coordinate the speakers and activities for the Clinical Engineering Week meeting. Serve on the review committee for applicants to the Clinical Engineering Internship Program. Prepare recruitment materials and assists in recruitment activities for applicants and host hospitals. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS MS Degree in Engineering. 5 Years’ experience working in the Clinical Engineering field at the manager or director level. Expertise in the Clinical Engineering field as demonstrated by oral presentations at national or regional Clinical Engineering meetings and / or published articles in conference proceedings or relative journals. Strong interpersonal, oral, and written communication skills. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Degrees or Certifications related to Clinical Engineering. 10+ Years’ experience working in the Clinical Engineering field at the manager or director level. APPOINTMENT TERMS The appointment term is one semester (approximately August 23 rd , 2026, to January 20 th , 2027). Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. TO APPLY Please submit a letter of application , resume , and the names and contact information of three professional references to Ms. Sowmya Ramesh ( sowmya.ramesh@uconn.edu ). Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. (HR26-24) Temporary CDL Drivers – Institute for the Brain and Cognitive Sciences – Posted June 11, 2026 Temporary CDL Drivers Institute for the Brain and Cognitive Sciences The Institute for the Brain and Cognitive Sciences (IBACS) seeks temporary CDL drivers to be part of a driver pool for a 2012 IHC Navistar LifeLine Mobile® Laboratory. This is a straight truck with an automatic transmission and air brakes. This driver position is responsible for round-trip transports of the mobile laboratory from the UConn Storrs Campus to a defined location in Connecticut and/or the greater New England area, with a focus on destinations that reach underserved communities and public gathering spaces. The exact destinations of the research and outreach events are subject to change. The driver must arrive in advance of the trip to inspect and if necessary, fuel the vehicle and change the water in the water reservoir. The driver is responsible for transporting the vehicle, with its contents, to a defined location where they will then be responsible for safely parking and stabilizing the vehicle and preparing its systems for use- such as deploying the bump-out and starting the diesel generator. The driver will remain on duty for the duration of the event and support the research and outreach team in using the vehicle’s systems. Upon completion of the event, the driver is responsible for preparing the vehicle for travel and safely returning the vehicle, along with its contents, to the UConn Storrs Campus. This is an on-call part-time truck driving job that is perfect for licensed CDL Class A or B drivers seeking supplemental income and looking for anywhere from 0 to 12 hours a week based on the Institute’s needs. Drivers with flexibility to any hours, including weekdays, weekday evenings and weekends are encouraged to apply. The truck will be reserved for events by research faculty and other UConn community members. To accommodate their varied schedules, our Institute is establishing a driver pool with multiple drivers. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Map out driving routes ahead of time to determine the most expedient trip and adjust the route to avoid heavy traffic or road construction, as needed. Prepare, drive, and safely operate the vehicle from the UConn Storrs Campus to a defined location. Pre- and post-inspect the truck. Ensure there is sufficient gas in the tank for the trip to and from the destination, and to run the generator for the needed timeframe. Stay on duty and remain with the vehicle during the research or outreach event. Assist the research and outreach teams with using the vehicle’s systems. Report any equipment issues. Other duties as required. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Relevant CDL. Experience operating similar vehicles for work purposes. Compliance with all road and transport rules. Ability to drive safely irrespective of the weather or time of day. TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment physical examination, including drug testing, as well as a criminal background check. Additionally, based on the CDL license requirement, employment of successful candidate will be contingent on a FMCSA Drug and Alcohol Clearinghouse review. The successful candidate may be required to participate in random drug testing. APPOINTMENT TERMS This is an on-call part-time truck driving job. Salary will be $35 per hour but is subject to change if the candidate is a retired State of Connecticut employee, to comply with rehired retiree employment regulations. TO APPLY Please submit a resume to Crystal Mills by email. For any questions, contact Crystal Mills, IBACS Coordinator. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce.  The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner.  This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all.  We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same.  All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, national origin, genetics, disability, age, or veteran status. The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO Employer. (HR26-22) 2025 Temporary University Specialist (Admissions Department) – Stamford Campus – Posted December 22, 2025 Temporary University Specialist Stamford Campus, Admissions Department The UConn Stamford Admissions Office is seeking a Temporary University Specialist to support administrative functions. Reporting to the Admissions Officer, this position is based at the Stamford Campus and will require evening and weekend hours. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Manage front office operations, including visitor interactions, appointment scheduling, answering phones, and admissions inquiries. Coordinate the master calendar to ensure proper scheduling and coverage. Organize and oversee high school and community-based organization visits, including outreach, agenda planning, and day-of logistics. Confirm scheduled tours, follow up with no-shows, and gather feedback from visitors. Update presentation materials regularly to meet the needs of various student populations and visit programs. Support major campus events such as Open Houses, UConn Bound Days, Student Panels, Shadow Day Programs, Specialty Major Programs and more. Act as a pre-submission resource for prospective students by supporting application preparation and readiness. Act as a knowledgeable resource for campus visitors, including prospective students and the general public. Represent UConn Stamford and the Admissions Department at local community events Provide campus and Residence Hall tours to students and guests. Utilize a Customer Relations Management System to check and confirm students’ application statuses, track missing materials, and record interactions. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree with one year of experience. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Strong interpersonal skills and the ability to work effectively with others. Proficiency with Microsoft Office products, especially Excel, Word, and Outlook. Willingness and the ability to work nights and weekends to support Admissions events and initiatives. Interest in working with high school and college students. Self-motivated with the ability to work autonomously. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Prior experience in a higher education setting. Demonstrated ability to plan, coordinate, and execute events. Knowledge of the admissions and recruitment cycle. APPOINTMENT TERMS This is a 6-month, in-person appointment for 35-40 hours per week, with the possibility of extension. The weekly schedule will vary based on campus and community events, requiring frequent evening and weekend commitments. The hourly rate is $20 – $25, commensurate with experience. TO APPLY Interested candidates should submit a resume , a letter of application that demonstrates how you meet the qualifications and requirements of this position, and contact information for three professional references with name, title, phone number, and email. Screening will begin immediately. Applications can be sent to carlos.lopez_rosas@uconn.edu. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce.  The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner.  This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all.  We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same.  All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, national origin, genetics, disability, age, or veteran status. The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO Employer. (HR25-52) Temporary University Specialist (Admissions Department) – Stamford Campus – Posted December 22, 2025 Temporary University Specialist St